WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.097
NET hosted services, Worker Services, IHostedService, and BackgroundService provide a common way to run work outside the direct request path.

2
00:00:09.097 --> 00:00:15.548
At a conceptual level, they give the application a startup phase, a running phase, and a stop phase.

3
00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:26.645
Those phases matter for security because they determine when configuration is loaded, when dependencies are created, when work begins, and how gracefully the service stops.

4
00:00:26.645 --> 00:00:32.967
Startup should validate required configuration and fail clearly when the worker cannot run safely.

5
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:44.322
Execution should be predictable: a queue consumer should process known message types, a timer should run on an understood interval, and a polling loop should have clear limits.

6
00:00:44.322 --> 00:00:53.612
Shutdown should respect cancellation and avoid leaving files, records, messages, or external operations in ambiguous states when the host stops.

7
00:00:53.612 --> 00:00:58.902
Dependency injection lifetimes deserve special attention in long-running services.

8
00:00:58.902 --> 00:01:07.160
A singleton worker that accidentally holds onto scoped services can create stale state, concurrency problems, or resource leaks.

9
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:13.031
A worker that creates scopes intentionally for each unit of work is easier to reason about.

10
00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:22.450
The lifecycle of database contexts, HTTP clients, repositories, validators, and transaction objects should match the unit of work being processed.

11
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:28.257
Scheduling, timers, polling loops, queue consumers, and recurring jobs all need ownership.

12
00:01:28.257 --> 00:01:38.966
A timer that overlaps with itself, a loop that runs without delay, or a consumer that prefetches more work than it can safely process can create operational pressure.

13
00:01:38.966 --> 00:01:41.547
These are not only performance concerns.

14
00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:47.224
They affect reliability, auditing, and the ability to recover when something goes wrong.

15
00:01:47.224 --> 00:01:51.805
Separating orchestration from business logic can improve reviewability.

16
00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:57.160
The worker can own scheduling, message receipt, cancellation, retries, and logging.

17
00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:02.579
A separate service can own validation, authorization checks, and business decisions.

18
00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:14.643
That division makes it easier to test the business behavior without reproducing the whole hosted-service runtime and easier to review the worker mechanics without reading all domain code.

19
00:02:14.643 --> 00:02:18.385
Avoid hidden global state and unclear execution ownership.

20
00:02:18.385 --> 00:02:29.933
Workers should document what starts them, what input they consume, which identity they use, what data they touch, how concurrency is controlled, and who responds when alerts fire.

21
00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:36.836
Before production deployment, reviewers should be able to explain the execution boundary in plain language.
